            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Perimeter and Area"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.121,
            0.403,
            0.141
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Making it ‘More’ or ‘Less’"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.145,
            0.833,
            0.188
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Observe these two figures. Is there any similarity or difference between the two?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.351,
            0.834,
            0.418
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Using 9 unit squares (having an area of 9 sq units), we have made figures with two different perimeters—the first figure has a perimeter of 12 units and the second has a perimeter of 20 units."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.42,
            0.835,
            0.51
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Arrange or draw different figures with 9 sq units to get other perimeters. Each square should align with at least one other square on at least one side completely and together all squares should form a single connected figure with no holes."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.117,
            0.516,
            0.594,
            0.538
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Using 9 unit squares, solve the following."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.54,
            0.619,
            0.561
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "1. What is the smallest perimeter possible?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.567,
            0.605,
            0.587
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2. What is the largest perimeter possible?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.593,
            0.641,
            0.614
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3. Make a figure with a perimeter of 18 units."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.62,
            0.833,
            0.685
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "4. Can you make other shaped figures for each of the above three perimeters, or is there only one shape with that perimeter? What is your reasoning?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.449,
            0.971,
            0.55,
            0.985
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Reprint 2026-27"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.689,
            0.834,
            0.733
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Let’s do something tricky now! We have a figure below having perimeter 24 units."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.738,
            0.502,
            0.851
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Without calculating all over again, observe, think and find out what will be the change in the perimeter if a new square is attached as shown on the right."
            }
          ]
        },
